ource limit. These are direct additions to the existing slice, not new component boundaries. They serve the VISION’s primary orientation question: which modules exist and what they own.\n\n**Limitations.** Kotlin and Scala source is not indexed; `cycles` and `stats` still use different directory-group populations; the relocated-project count is not rendered. The builder reported running DAP-C05 as a monitored background task despite the packet’s foreground instruction. Its log reaches `DAP-C05-OK`, and the builder reports the owned run directory and worktree removed; I record the process deviation rather than presenting it as an independent run.\n\nManager interpretation by manager-claude-fable-5-1: Bookkeeper attempts 1 and 2 (identical) transcribed the reviewer-owned DAP-C06 as relied-on-builder-evidence, which the owner-aware rule (TD-026) refuses for an accepted reviewer-owned inspection check. The reviewer's raw report states the inspection itself: \"DAP-C06 is OBSERVED PASS by source inspection of the six-path diff, the scanner admission rule and the ownership filter.\" Mechanical repair of that one field to the verification kind the report states (reproduced, passed, the report's sentences as supporting evidence) — the same repair as IUR-C07, TSA-C05, DGC-B05 and MDSS-C05; every other assessment, the result (accepted), the zero findings, the zero decisions and the report are the bookkeeper's transcription of the reviewer's output, unchanged. No conversion of meaning. Source provider run review-DGC-ATTRIBUTION-PRECISE-1-0; retained output .agent-manager/slices/DGC-ATTRIBUTION-PRECISE-1/review-0.json sha256:1e137c3214fb0012f8e2a57a3f93c98aef4e8c150eb2e14d789108eef08de95a. The provider remains the performer; this interpretation is not independent execution evidence."
